WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:09.090
Gravity is a force. It pulls things
down, and keeps us on the ground.

2
00:00:09.310 --> 00:00:12.329
Friction is a force too.
It makes moving harder.

3
00:00:12.829 --> 00:00:17.194
A forcemeter measures a force.
It is a spring with a hook.

4
00:00:17.414 --> 00:00:22.569
Hang an apple on it. Gravity pulls
it down, and the spring stretches.

5
00:00:22.789 --> 00:00:27.619
The scale tells you the force in
newtons. This one reads two newtons.

6
00:00:27.839 --> 00:00:32.204
Hang the shoe: three newtons.
Hang the big book: five newtons.

7
00:00:32.424 --> 00:00:37.533
The heavier the thing, the harder gravity
pulls it, and the further the spring stretches.

8
00:00:37.753 --> 00:00:45.183
A forcemeter does not measure grams. It measures
the pull, in newtons. The pull is gravity.

9
00:00:45.683 --> 00:00:49.909
Let go of a ball and it falls.
Nobody pushed it. Gravity pulled it.

10
00:00:50.129 --> 00:00:53.612
Gravity pulls everything
towards the centre of the Earth.

11
00:00:53.832 --> 00:00:57.408
That is what down means.
Down is towards the centre.

12
00:00:57.628 --> 00:01:00.647
On the other side of the world,
down points the other way.

13
00:01:00.867 --> 00:01:06.021
The child there is not upside down. Wherever
you stand, down points to the centre.

14
00:01:06.521 --> 00:01:11.165
Push a block along the floor. It
slows down, and then it stops.

15
00:01:11.385 --> 00:01:14.822
What made it slow down and
stop? Friction did that.

16
00:01:15.042 --> 00:01:18.571
Friction is a force between
two surfaces that rub together.

17
00:01:18.791 --> 00:01:22.739
It pushes back on the moving
block. It makes moving harder.

18
00:01:23.239 --> 00:01:27.372
Rough surfaces make more friction.
Smooth surfaces make less.

19
00:01:27.592 --> 00:01:31.307
Push a block on ice, on smooth
wood and on rough carpet.

20
00:01:31.527 --> 00:01:36.496
Same block, same push. Only the
surface changes: a fair test.

21
00:01:36.716 --> 00:01:40.153
On the ice it slides a
long way: nine marks.

22
00:01:40.373 --> 00:01:45.017
On the wood, six marks. On the
carpet it stops soonest: only two.

23
00:01:45.237 --> 00:01:49.927
Even ice has a little friction. The
block still slows down and stops.

24
00:01:50.427 --> 00:01:56.279
Write your three distances in a
table: ice nine, wood six, carpet two.

25
00:01:56.499 --> 00:02:00.307
Then draw them as bars against
a scale. That is a bar chart.

26
00:02:00.527 --> 00:02:05.681
The scale up the side counts the
marks: one, two, three, up to nine.

27
00:02:05.901 --> 00:02:10.778
The tallest bar is the longest
slide. That means the least friction.

28
00:02:10.998 --> 00:02:16.338
Read the bars, and you see the pattern:
smoother surface, longer slide.

29
00:02:16.838 --> 00:02:20.600
A forcemeter measures a force.
It reads the pull in newtons.

30
00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:24.628
Gravity pulls things down,
towards the centre of the Earth.

31
00:02:24.848 --> 00:02:28.563
Friction makes moving harder.
Rough surfaces make more of it.

32
00:02:28.783 --> 00:02:34.310
A bar chart shows the pattern at a
glance: smoother surface, longer slide.
